  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nA weak frontal boundary will push southward and stall out over \ncentral Kansas on Wednesday into Thursday morning with no weather \neffects other than a wind shift. High confidence for warm above\nnormal temperatures to prevail over the region during the next \n7 days with upper level high pressure in control. Models do show\na tropical system developing in the Gulf of Mexico this week \nand being pulled northward towards Illinois/Indiana over the \nweekend. This current model track would have little to no \nweather impacts for Kansas with a continuation of warm and dry \nweather conditions. Heading into early next week longer range \nmodels show upper level pattern becoming more amplified with \ndeepening trough over the western states, and upper level ridge \nover the central states. This will cause southerly winds to \nincrease along with deeper moisture beginning to stream \nnorthward from west Texas into western Kansas for Monday- \nTuesday. There is low confidence on model timing for when the \nupper trough over the southwest states shifts eastward across \nthe Rockies with associated precipitation chances spreading into\ncentral Kansas next week.",
